questions.question:
You have an accounts table (id, balance). Two concurrent transactions run this application-level logic to withdraw money:

-- Transaction A
BEGIN;
SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 1; -- returns 100
-- app computes new_balance = balance - 50
UPDATE accounts SET balance = 50 WHERE id = 1;
COMMIT;

-- Transaction B (runs concurrently)
BEGIN;
SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 1; -- also returns 100
-- app computes new_balance = balance - 30
UPDATE accounts SET balance = 70 WHERE id = 1;
COMMIT;

Explain the bug that can occur here and provide a minimal code change (SQL) to prevent the lost-update when these transactions run concurrently. Keep the fix simple and entry-level.

questions.expected_answer:
Bug: a lost update occurs because both transactions read the same initial balance and then write based on stale reads (read-then-write without synchronization). Minimal fixes (any one is acceptable):

Option 1 — row lock (pessimistic): Acquire a lock when reading so the second transaction waits:
BEGIN;
SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 1 FOR UPDATE; -- locks row
-- app computes new_balance = balance - 50
UPDATE accounts SET balance = new_balance WHERE id = 1;
COMMIT;

Option 2 — atomic update (no separate read): let the DB do the arithmetic so writes are not based on stale client state:
BEGIN;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 50 WHERE id = 1;
COMMIT;

Option 3 — optimistic check: include the old value in WHERE and verify one row affected:
BEGIN;
-- assuming old_balance is what the app read
UPDATE accounts SET balance = 50 WHERE id = 1 AND balance = 100;
-- check affected_rows = 1 else retry
COMMIT;

Any of these prevents the lost-update; the simplest is using SELECT ... FOR UPDATE or using an atomic UPDATE statement.

questions.user_answer:
BEGIN;
SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = 1 FOR UPDATE;
-- app computes new_balance = balance - X
